# Example: a global Claude Code CLAUDE.md for thismoon users

An example `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (user-global instructions, applied to every
project) tuned for someone running the `*.this` platform locally. It wires
Claude Code to the thismoon MCP servers — csl, worklog, humanizer, events,
deps, present, reminder, keep, bionic — and sets sensible defaults for commits,
PRs, code search, and agent discipline.

Copy the parts you want into your own `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. Everything here is
portable: no machine-private hostnames, secrets, or private tracker IDs.

Each MCP server this file references is a component in this repo:

| Component | Source | What it backs here |
|-----------|--------|--------------------|
| csl | [`services/csl`](../../services/csl) | code search (`csl_*`) |
| events | [`services/events`](../../services/events) | event log (`events_*`) |
| deps | [`services/deps`](../../services/deps) | dependency scanning (`deps_*`) |
| present | [`services/present`](../../services/present) | briefing pages (`present_*`) |
| reminder | [`services/reminder`](../../services/reminder) | reminders (`reminder_*`) |
| keeper-of-facts | [`services/keeper-of-facts`](../../services/keeper-of-facts) | assertion store (`kof_*`) |
| worklog | [`tools/worklog`](../../tools/worklog) | cross-session work state (`worklog_*`) |
| humanizer | [`tools/humanizer`](../../tools/humanizer) | AI-writing detection (`humanizer_*`) |
| bionic | [`tools/bionic`](../../tools/bionic) | bionic-reading render (`bionic_*`) |

Skill references like `/golang-pro`, `/simplify`, `/handoff`, and `/plan` are
your own Claude Code skills — not shipped by this repo.

---

## This File
- Keep this file under version control (a dotfiles repo) and symlink it into
  place: `ln -s ~/dotfiles/claude/CLAUDE.md ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. Resolve the
  real path with `readlink ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, then edit it in the repo so
  changes are tracked.

## Web Fallback
- If WebSearch or WebFetch fails (blocked, timeout, empty, incomplete), retry
  using agent-browser.

## Commits
- Conventional commits format.
- **NEVER add Claude as co-author.** Strip any
  `Co-Authored-By: Claude ... <noreply@anthropic.com>` line from commit
  messages and PR bodies before committing, even when a generated template
  includes it.

## Git & PR Workflow
- NEVER add a testplan section to PR descriptions.
- NEVER add a runbook section to PR descriptions.
- PR descriptions: summary of changes + issue/ticket ID if provided, nothing else.
- When using the `gh` CLI, use the repo's GitHub PR template if one exists
  (check `.github/`).

## Model Selection & Context Efficiency
**Keep the main context lean — delegate search-heavy work to subagents and
receive summaries back.** Subagents protect the main context from large tool
results (file dumps, search output, MCP responses). Direct tool use in the main
context is fine for targeted reads and writes — avoid broad searches that dump
hundreds of lines into context.

**Agent discipline:**
- **Scale to task complexity.** Single-file or few-line changes: no plan mode,
  no task tracking, no subagents — just do it. Multi-file changes: light task
  tracking. Architectural or cross-repo work: plan mode + agents.
- **Batch related reads into fewer agents.** Don't spawn one agent per file —
  give one agent a broader mandate. 3 focused agents beat 12 narrow ones.
- **Never run multiple write agents against the same file.** Concurrent edits to
  shared files cause merge conflicts and broken code. Serialize writes.
- **Include failure history in follow-up agent prompts.** When re-attempting
  something that already failed, tell the new agent what was tried and why it
  failed. Fresh agents repeat dead ends.
- **Don't launch wide agent fan-outs without checking scope.** For a narrow
  question, go direct.
- **Subagents do NOT inherit this file — propagate the conventions into their
  prompt.** Spawned agents start without these instructions, so restate the
  relevant rules inline: (1) use `csl_search`/`csl_read` for code search in
  indexed repos, not bash grep/find; (2) never commit/push or push to main —
  return a diff/summary and let the main agent handle git. Keep it to the rules
  the agent's task actually touches.

## Context Management
- Run /compact proactively — don't wait until context is full.
- At 150K tokens: mention that context is getting large.
- At 250K, 350K, and 450K tokens: explicitly ask whether to compact before
  continuing.
- After completing any discrete task (feature shipped, PR merged, review done),
  split to a new session rather than carrying context forward.
- Subagents return findings concisely — no raw file dumps, no full search
  blocks. Summaries only, with file paths and key snippets.
- After any Agent-heavy phase (5+ agents in one turn), consider compacting
  before the next phase.

## Worklog — resumable cross-session work state
For long tasks that span multiple sessions and multiple repos (often started
from a tmp dir), use **worklog** (a thismoon tool) to keep "where I am" in a
stable, searchable place keyed by **ticket id or topic** — not by working
directory. This is the fix for work that auto-memory loses because it started
in a tmp dir.

- **Store:** `~/code/worklog/`, one directory per task (`<key>/CONTEXT.md` +
  lazy `repos/<repo>.md`). Local-only git, no remote — resume on the machine you
  left. csl indexes it.
- **Prefer the worklog MCP tools** (`worklog_checkpoint`, `worklog_list`,
  `worklog_search`, `worklog_show`, `worklog_status`); the `worklog` CLI is the
  fallback. When checkpointing via MCP, pass `cwd` so the touched repo is
  recorded.
- **Triggers** (`/worklog` skill): "worklog", "checkpoint", "save my progress",
  "where was I", "pick up where I left off". One task spanning N repos is **one
  item** with N per-repo notes.
- **Cold-start principle:** the checkpoint `where` field is the ONLY context a
  fresh session gets. Write it for a reader with zero prior knowledge: goal,
  repo paths, conclusions with evidence, working tool/query examples with exact
  parameters, anti-patterns that failed, decisions and why, links to
  tickets/docs/PRs, and concrete next steps. A thin checkpoint forces the next
  session to re-discover everything.
- Worklog holds resumable task state. "Remember" still routes to auto-memory
  (durable atomic facts); `/handoff` writes a one-off bridge doc.

## keep — assertion store (evidence-pinned session knowledge)
keeper-of-facts, short kof (a thismoon service, web at `http://kof.this/`) stores **assertions**:
one-sentence statements about how a system behaves, each pinned to a hashed
file line-range. `kof check` flips assertions stale when the pinned code
changes, so unlike docs or memory they decay detectably.

- **Consult before spelunking:** starting non-trivial work in a repo, run
  `kof_query` with a `subject` prefix (`repo:<org>/<repo>...`) —
  `verified`/fresh assertions are pre-derived understanding with evidence;
  `kof_get` shows the pins.
- **Deposit after deriving:** when a session works out something non-obvious
  that took real effort (behavior, invariant, dead end), store it with
  `kof_assert` — at least one evidence pin (`repo_path`, `file`,
  `start_line`, `end_line`) is required, one sentence per assertion, subject
  namespaced like `repo:<org>/<repo>/path`.
- **Correct, don't tolerate:** an assertion that proves wrong gets
  `kof_retract` with a note (the counter-evidence). Treat `stale` assertions
  as unverified hints until re-checked.
- **Division of labor:** auto-memory = durable facts about you and your
  projects; worklog = resumable task state; keep = evidence-pinned claims
  about code behavior that go stale with the code.

## Language & Tool Preferences
- Go for CLI tools and infrastructure tooling (install to `~/code/bin`, use
  `go env GOBIN`/`GOPATH`).
- Python 3 for agents/bots/services — use `pip3` not `pip`.
- Use /golang-pro for Go code review and implementation.
- Use /simplify for code quality passes.
- Use /humanizer for documentation and external-facing text.

## Local Code Search — Global Default (csl)
**Use `csl_*` MCP tools for repo discovery AND code search** — repo lookup,
health checks, file reading, symbol search, and pattern matching. csl
(`code-search-local`, a thismoon service) indexes your local checkouts with a
zoekt index that is faster than filesystem grep and returns structured results
that keep context lean.

- Do NOT use the `csl` CLI directly — the MCP tools and CLI share the same
  foundation, so if one is down the other is too.
- **Prefer `csl_search`/`csl_read` over Bash grep/find/rg when the repo is
  indexed.** Check with `csl_repo_info` or `csl_repo_lookup` first. If the repo
  is not indexed, Bash grep/find/rg is the correct fallback. Bash grep is also
  fine for non-search uses (piping, filtering command output, one-off checks on
  a single known file).

Available `csl_*` MCP tools:
- **Repo:** `csl_repo_lookup`, `csl_repo_info`, `csl_repo_pull`,
  `csl_repo_reindex`.
- **Search (lexical):** `csl_search`, `csl_count`, `csl_read`,
  `csl_query_validate`.
- **Search (semantic/hybrid):** `csl_semantic_search` (vector, by meaning),
  `csl_hybrid_search` (RRF fusion of lexical + semantic — default when you want
  exact-match safety AND meaning-based recall).

**zoekt query best practices:**
- Use 1–2 search terms max, then narrow with `repo:`/`f:`/`lang:` filters. AND
  requires all terms in the same file — 3+ terms almost always returns zero.
- OR syntax: use `|` with no spaces (`foo|bar`). Uppercase `OR` is a literal
  string. Spaces around `|` break it.
- Filter prefixes: `repo:` (not `r:`), `f:` (not `file:`). Prefer the dedicated
  tool params over inline filter syntax.
- If a query returns zero results, run it through `csl_query_validate` to see
  how zoekt parsed it.
- Verify the repo is indexed before searching: `csl_repo_lookup` first.

**Grep-to-zoekt translation** (muscle memory is grep):
- `grep -r "foo"` → `foo`
- `grep -r "foo bar"` → `foo.*bar` (regex for proximity, not exact-match)
- `grep --include="*.go"` → `f:.*\.go$` (regex not glob)
- `grep -v test` → `-test` or `-f:([Tt]est)`
- `grep -E "foo\|bar"` → `foo|bar` (no spaces around pipe)
- `find . -name "*.go" | xargs grep foo` → `foo f:.*\.go$`

## Repo Discovery
- **Use `csl_repo_lookup` or `csl_repo_info` to find repos.** Don't use `find`,
  `ls`, `Glob`, or `Bash` to hunt for repo directories.
- `csl_repo_info` returns git health state (branch, dirty files, index
  staleness, suggested action). Use it before starting work to check if a repo
  needs commit/stash/pull/reindex.
- If lookup returns empty, the repo is not checked out locally — say so, don't
  guess paths.
- Use `csl_repo_pull` before creating branches on repos that may be out of date.
- Use `csl_repo_reindex` after significant changes so `csl_search` stays current.

## Repo Freshness Protocol
- **Peek mode:** exploring, orienting, browsing — just use
  `csl_search`/`csl_read`/`csl_repo_info`. No pull needed. State results as
  "based on local checkout, may be behind upstream."
- **Conclusion mode:** before acting on repo state (code changes, conclusions,
  PRs, plans), run `csl_repo_info` first. If dirty: STOP and ask — don't assume
  what to preserve. If on a non-default branch: ask before switching. If stale:
  ask before pulling. If clean and current: proceed. Re-verify after any pull.
- Same protocol applies to subagents — tell them which mode they're in.

## Multi-Repo Awareness
- When changing working directory to a different git repo, read the repo-root
  `CLAUDE.md` (and `.claude/CLAUDE.md` if it exists) before making changes.
- Per-repo CLAUDE.md instructions take precedence over global for repo-specific
  concerns (build commands, conventions, test frameworks).
- If a repo has no CLAUDE.md, fall back to global only.

## Domain Docs — CONTEXT.md & ADRs
Repos may carry two domain docs alongside CLAUDE.md. Read both before working in
an area.

- **Division of labor:** CLAUDE.md says how to work in the repo (commands,
  conventions, invariants). `CONTEXT.md` is the domain glossary — canonical
  terms with tight definitions, `_Avoid_:` lists of banned synonyms,
  relationships between concepts. `docs/adr/NNNN-slug.md` records why
  hard-to-reverse decisions went the way they did.
- **Use the glossary's vocabulary** in code, tests, comments, commits, and docs.
  If wording conflicts with the glossary, call it out instead of guessing.
- **Respect ADRs.** Before changing code in an area, check `docs/adr/` for
  decisions that touch it. Never "fix" a deliberate deviation an ADR records —
  surface it and ask first.
- **Multi-context repos** have a root `CONTEXT-MAP.md` pointing at per-context
  `CONTEXT.md` files.

## Change Impact Analysis — before code changes
Before a code change, answer: (1) what breaks — call sites, configs, tests,
downstream code? Search, don't assume. (2) what happens to existing data —
persisted state under the old shape? (3) what happens to existing consumers —
silent breaking change? (4) is it reversible?

State findings before editing — e.g. "this renames a public function used in 4
other files; I'll update them all." One-liner for trivial changes; explicit
blast radius for public APIs, schemas, persisted data, or shared config.

For irreversible or external operations (migrations, deployments, shared-config
changes), explain the action and expected outcome BEFORE executing. Wait for
confirmation. Don't silently start destructive or external ops.

## Design Principles
- DRY (Rule of Three), YAGNI, KISS — apply strictly.
- Pure functions; push side effects (I/O, state, time) to the edges — functional
  core, imperative shell.
- Immutable by default. Plain data + functions over stateful objects.
- Make illegal states unrepresentable; parse, don't validate at boundaries.
- Single responsibility — small functions, one job. If naming needs "and", split.
- Compose small pieces; don't abstract before the third use.
- Pass dependencies in, don't construct them inside — for testability.
- Explicit over implicit — clear names, explicit errors, no hidden global state.
- Fail fast.
- Prefer existing libraries/patterns; read before writing. Methods under 20
  lines. No swallowed exceptions.
- Single source of truth — no duplicate configs/skills across repos.
- Use absolute paths when referencing files across projects.

## Testing
- TDD (red-green-refactor) — especially for refactors: pin behavior in a test
  before changing code.
- Test behavior, not implementation — survives refactors.
- Arrange-Act-Assert; Test Pyramid (unit-heavy, few e2e); FIRST as hygiene.
- Add unit tests for new code.

## YAGNI
- No abstractions without 2–3 real use cases.
- No "what if we also need" — we don't.
- Simple solutions first. Refactor when actual complexity arrives.

## Never Accept
- Methods longer than 50 lines.
- Duplicated validation logic.
- Magic numbers without explanation.
- Tests that test implementation details rather than behavior.
- "Unrelated" test failures — main is always green. If tests fail, fix them.

## Response Style
- Keep responses concise and technical — no AI-sounding fluff.
- Don't summarize what you just did at the end of responses.
- **Trust user runtime observations.** When the user reports a live error, don't
  argue the code looks correct. Terminal output is ground truth. Investigate
  from there.
- **When a tool or API can't provide what's needed, say so immediately.** Don't
  retry the same failing approach — state the limitation and suggest an
  alternative.

## Plan Mode Behavior
- **When the user asks a question while in plan mode:** answer directly. Do NOT
  re-present the plan. If the answer changes the plan, update it silently.
- After answering, follow through on whatever the question implies.
- Only when the conversation settles and the plan is ready, ask: "Want me to
  show the updated plan?"

## Documentation & Writing Style
- **Zero AI-writing patterns in committed text.** Run text through humanizer (a
  thismoon tool). `humanizer_detect` for short-form text; the `/humanizer`
  skill for docs and multi-paragraph prose. Key MCP tools: `humanizer_detect`,
  `humanizer_voice_profile`, `humanizer_voice_diff`.
- **First-use rule for abbreviations:** the first time a shortened name appears
  in a document, spell out the full name followed by the short form in
  parentheses, then use the short form everywhere after. Applies per-document.
- Well-known acronyms (HTTP, JSON, DNS, API, CLI, URL, YAML, SQL, TLS, SSH)
  don't need expansion.
- **Pre-draft clarification for RFCs and design docs.** Before a first draft,
  ask 2–3 targeted questions: what must NOT appear, what framing/tone is
  expected, who the audience is.

## Research Workflow
- When research is needed, use /plan and agent teams — assume research tasks are
  complex by default. One agent per repo for multi-repo work.
- Research before implementing: understand the problem space first, then build.
- **Confirm the data model and core algorithm before building new components.**
  Getting the data model wrong means a full rework, not a tweak.
- **When architectural choices are still open, surface them before writing
  code.** Raise the decision first; don't implement option A and then discover
  the user wanted an RFC exploring A through E.

## Glossary

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| csl | code-search-local (thismoon service) |
| keeper-of-facts | assertion store with evidence pins (thismoon service) |
| worklog | resumable cross-session work state (thismoon tool) |
| humanizer | AI-writing detector/rewriter (thismoon tool) |
| ralph | recipe-driven local service manager |
| t-man / d-man | thismoon platform foundations (service manager, front door) |
